{"id": "whjungfrau", "name": "Swiss Alps Jungfrau-Aletsch", "short": "Jungfrau-Aletsch", "place": "Switzerland", "lat": 46.5, "lon": 8.0, "side": "r", "cont": "Europe", "country": "Switzerland", "kind": "Polar & mountain", "park": true, "blurb": "Ibex, marmots and eagles around the longest glacier in the Alps.", "tip": {"id": "marmot", "title": "Thin out the marmots", "text": "Marmots are a staple of golden eagles, foxes and stoats. Set Alpine marmot to -100% and watch those predators and the hares they turn to."}, "sources": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Swiss Alps Jungfrau-Aletsch", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swiss_Alps_Jungfrau-Aletsch"}, {"title": "Wikipedia: Alpine ibex", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alpine_ibex"}, {"title": "Wikipedia: Alpine marmot", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alpine_marmot"}, {"title": "Wikipedia: Golden eagle", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Golden_eagle"}, {"title": "Wikipedia: Chamois", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chamois"}, {"title": "Wikipedia: Alpine chough", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alpine_chough"}, {"title": "Wikipedia: Swiss pine", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swiss_pine"}], "notes": "The species list comes from GBIF occurrence records inside a circle around the park, so it can include nearby areas. Feeding links come from GloBI records and the sources listed where they state them; others are general ecology and are illustrative.", "imgwiki": "Swiss Alps Jungfrau-Aletsch", "icon": "ice", "tier": "overview", "nodes": [{"id": "sun", "sp": "sunlight", "xh": 0.08, "desc": "Short alpine summers and intense light at altitude set how long plants can grow on the high slopes."}, {"id": "snow", "sp": "snowpack", "xh": 0.3}, {"id": "glacier", "sp": "glacier meltwater", "xh": 0.52}, {"id": "soil", "sp": "alpine soils", "xh": 0.72}, {"id": "decomp", "sp": "decomposer community", "xh": 0.92, "desc": "Break down dead plants, animals and carcasses, returning nutrients to the soil."}, {"id": "stonepine", "sp": "pinus cembra", "xh": 0.12}, {"id": "spruce", "sp": "picea abies", "xh": 0.38, "desc": "The dominant subalpine conifer on the south side of the site. Its shoots and needles are winter food for chamois and ibex."}, {"id": "meadow", "sp": "festuca", "xh": 0.62, "name": "Alpine meadow plants", "desc": "Grasses and herbs of the alpine meadows above the treeline, the main summer food of marmots, chamois and ibex."}, {"id": "rhodo", "sp": "rhododendron ferrugineum", "xh": 0.86}, {"id": "nutcracker", "sp": "nucifraga caryocatactes", "xh": 0.06, "name": "Spotted nutcracker", "wiki": "Spotted nutcracker", "desc": "Eats Swiss stone pine seeds and spreads them, since the heavy seeds are barely wind-dispersed."}, {"id": "chough", "sp": "pyrrhocorax graculus", "xh": 0.18, "desc": "Picks insects from alpine pasture in summer and fruit in winter. Flocks gather around huts and ski areas for scraps.", "ic": "meadowlark"}, {"id": "apollo", "sp": "parnassius apollo", "xh": 0.3}, {"id": "marmot", "sp": "marmota marmota", "xh": 0.42, "badge": "Ecosystem engineer"}, {"id": "hare", "sp": "lepus timidus", "xh": 0.54, "ic": "vole"}, {"id": "deer", "sp": "cervus elaphus", "xh": 0.66, "name": "Red deer", "wiki": "Red deer", "desc": "The largest herbivore of the valleys and forest edges, grazing grasses and browsing shrubs and shoots.", "ic": "elk"}, {"id": "chamois", "sp": "rupicapra rupicapra", "xh": 0.78, "desc": "Grazes alpine meadows in summer and moves into forest in winter, eating conifer needles and bark. Lynx and wolves are its natural predators."}, {"id": "ibex", "sp": "capra ibex", "xh": 0.9}, {"id": "stoat", "sp": "mustela erminea", "xh": 0.2, "desc": "A small hunter of the mountains that takes voles, hares and young marmots."}, {"id": "fox", "sp": "vulpes vulpes", "xh": 0.5, "wiki": "Red fox", "desc": "Widespread hunter and scavenger from valley to alpine meadows, taking hares, marmots, birds and carrion."}, {"id": "raven", "sp": "corvus corax", "xh": 0.8, 